In new Lantern code, use **sqlx** — diesel is fully retired (switched in March 2026, last diesel module removed in PR #188), so don't add any diesel usage (memory note `384c9ad9`).

Practical guidance:

1. **Write plain SQL, compile-time checked.** In sqlx you write actual SQL strings (or use the `.sqlx` offline-query files) rather than a query-builder DSL. If you use the offline feature, run `sqlx prepare` after changing queries so the compile-time checks see the schema.

2. **Async style.** The move to sqlx was specifically for async support, so use its async API:

   ```rust
   // query returning rows
   let users = sqlx::query_as!(
       User,
       "SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE active = true ORDER BY name"
   )
   .fetch_all(&pool)
   .await?;

   // single row
   let user = sqlx::query_as!(
       User,
       "SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE id = $1",
       user_id
   )
   .fetch_one(&pool)
   .await?;
   ```

   (If you skip `.sqlx`/offline mode, fall back to `sqlx::query` with manual macro bindings, but prefer the checked variants.)

3. **Don't forget schema changes.** If the query needs a new column or table, add a migration rather than editing schema ad hoc: `sqlx migrate add -r <name>` (reversible), lives in `migrations/`, apply with `sqlx migrate run`. Never edit an already-applied migration (memory note `6542b91e`). A PR touching migrations will need Chen Bo in addition to the default reviewer @priya-r (memory note `8abc0182`).

If you tell me what you're querying (tables, purpose), I can start a workflow to draft the actual query code and, if needed, the migration.
